<%# This screen drives htmx, so the layout loads it. Set here rather than in the routes
    because here is where the hx- attributes are: a partial that stops using htmx, or one
    that starts, changes its own answer in the same edit. A route flag would be a second
    place to remember, and the failure it produces is silent -- buttons that quietly stop
    swapping and fall back to full page posts. The same shape as @charts, which every view
    that plots something sets for the same reason. %>
<%- @htmx = true %>
<%# The swappable core of the session view. Every form posts with htmx targeting
    #session-body, so a tap re-renders progress, the set row, and the RPE state
    together; without JS the same forms post normally and the route redirects. %>
<%# One segment per lift, not per set. A set is the wrong unit for a bar: five movements
    of six sets drew thirty dashes twelve pixels wide on a phone, which is a barcode, and
    it disagreed with the panel directly beneath it saying "1 of 5" over five dots. The
    bar now counts what the session is made of, which is what #176 asks for.

    A segment fills with its own sets rather than flipping whole, so a lift four sets into
    six is two thirds lime and reads as underway -- the distinction an all-or-nothing
    segment throws away, and the one that matters most while you are in the middle of a
    lift. The slices are the sets themselves, laid out with no gap inside a rounded,
    clipped track, so they read as one bar filling rather than as six dashes. That keeps
    every set the old bar showed and needs no width computed into a style attribute or an
    arbitrary class -- either of which would have to survive htmx swapping this partial
    out from under the CDN's JIT on every tap. %>
<%# data-lift-segment marks each segment with the lift it belongs to, so the script at the
    foot of session.erb can say which one you are looking at. The bar answers "how much of
    this session is done"; on its own it could not answer "and where am I in it", which is
    the question a swipe asks -- the bar sat perfectly still while the screen moved, which
    is #212. Marking the current segment makes one bar answer both.

    Which panel is centred is a scroll position, and CSS cannot see one, so this is set from
    JS. It degrades to the bar exactly as it was: without the script every segment is
    unmarked, which is what it looked like before, and the per-panel dots and "3 of 5" are
    rendered server-side and go on saying where you are regardless. %>
<%# Rendered on its own as well as inside the body, because completing a set changes the
    bar and the count without changing anything else on the page. When it comes back on its
    own it carries hx-swap-oob, which tells htmx to find the element with this id and
    replace it wherever it is, rather than putting it where the response was aimed. That is
    what lets a tap send back one lift panel and still have the bar agree with it (#235).

    `oob` is a local with a default so the two callers read the same: the body renders it
    plainly, the complete route renders it with oob: true. %>
<div id="session-progress"<%== ' hx-swap-oob="true"' if defined?(oob) && oob %> class="sticky top-0 bg-gray-100 py-2">
  <div class="flex gap-0.5" aria-label="Session progress">
    <%- session_lifts.each_with_index do |lift, position| %>
      <div data-lift-segment="<%= position %>" class="flex h-2 flex-1 overflow-hidden rounded-sm bg-gray-300">
        <%- lift.each do |set| %>
          <div class="flex-1 <%= set[:is_completed] ? 'bg-lime-500' : '' %>"></div>
        <%- end %>
      </div>
    <%- end %>
  </div>
  <%# Sets rather than lifts, and deliberately: the bar is now the answer to "how many
      movements", so the line under it is where the exact set count still has to live. %>
  <%# The clock joins it rather than taking a line of its own (#281). This paragraph is the
      session's status line and two short facts fit on it; a second line would push the
      first Done button further down every screen for a number nobody reads mid-set.

      Inside this <p> and not in a flex row around it, because spec/session_progress_spec.rb
      anchors its reader on the literal `<p class="mt-1` that closes the bar -- markup that
      moved the tag would make that spec read an empty string and pass for the wrong reason.
      A middot between the two, the same separator views/programs/show.erb already uses.

      Server-rendered first and ticked by script afterwards. The numbers are true as sent,
      so a browser with JavaScript off shows the session length as it was at page load
      rather than nothing at all -- and this element comes back on every tap and every poll,
      which re-baselines it without the script having to.

      data-since is epoch seconds for the script to count from, and it is the *server's*
      idea of now subtracted out: the attribute carries how many seconds have already
      elapsed, so a phone whose clock is wrong still counts correctly from what it was
      told. aria-hidden on the ticking part, because a screen reader announcing a number
      that changes every second is a screen reader nobody can use. %>
  <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-gray-500">
    <%= @sets.count { |set| set[:is_completed] } %> of <%= @sets.length %> sets
    <%- if @timing && @timing[:overall] %>
      <span aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span>
      <span id="session-clock" data-elapsed="<%= @timing[:overall] %>"><%= Timing.phrase(@timing[:overall]) %></span>
      <%- if @timing[:overall_basis] == :in_progress %>
        <span>so far</span>
      <%- end %>
    <%- end %>
  </p>
</div>
